    "message": "feat(admin): Model Routing reads as a route, not a grid of fields\n\nThe layout contradicted the data. Model Routing rendered into the generic\nauto-fit field grid, so a tier's primary model and its fallback chain landed\nin separate, often non-adjacent, cells: \"Opus Override\" finished row one and\n\"Opus Fallback Chain\" started row two. The one relationship that governs\nevery request -- what this tier tries, and in what order -- was invisible.\nAround it sat roughly 200px of dead space per row and nine near-identical\nhelp sentences, while the only thing actually truncated was the model name.\n\nEach tier is now one card, and its models descend a vertical rail: a filled\ndot for the primary, numbered markers for each fallback, terminating in the\nadd affordance. The rail's length is the depth of that tier's safety net --\nno fallbacks gives a stub, three gives a long descent. Numbering earns its\nplace here because the order is the try-order, not decoration.\n\nThe rail is drawn per node rather than as one absolute line down the card.\nA single line with a fixed offset only meets the markers while every row is\nexactly the same height, which is not a guarantee worth depending on; each\nnode carrying its own segment is exact at any height. The markers are raised\nabove it, because absolutely positioned segments otherwise paint over the\nstatic content they are supposed to run behind.\n\nCopy: the nine repeated sentences are gone. \"Select None to use the Default\nModel for X requests\" x4 becomes a state the card reports -- Inherits default\nor Custom route -- and \"Tried in order when X cannot serve a request\" x5 is\nstated once above the grid. Repeated near-identical text teaches the eye to\nskip the whole column. The default card gets no state chip at all: it is the\nthing the others inherit, so calling it custom would be noise on every\ninstall.\n\nModel refs are set in monospace. They are paths, not prose, and mono makes\nthe provider prefix scannable instead of making a long slug look like a\nmistake. Adds --font-mono; the palette is untouched, since this is one\nsection of a shipped dashboard and repainting it would be vandalism.\n\nThe vision adapter is deliberately not a sixth route card. It fires on what\na request contains rather than on which model was asked for, so it gets its\nown shape below the grid instead of masquerading as a tier.\n\nStructurally this only changes arrangement: buildFieldControl() is factored\nout of renderField() so both the generic grid and the routing view wire\ncontrols into the same dirty/apply machinery, and the rail carries the\n`field` class so every existing input style still applies. Any field the\nmanifest adds to this section later still renders, in a plain grid below.\n\nVerification: 1066 tests pass; ruff, ty and node --check clean. Live server\non a throwaway HOME serves the page and all eleven routing fields with the\nboilerplate descriptions removed. Cross-checked mechanically that every\nroute-* class the JS emits has a CSS rule and vice versa, which caught a dead\nclass and an animation that was defined but never applied.\n\nNot verified: this has not been rendered in a browser -- there is no browser\ntooling in this environment, so the visual result is reasoned about, not\nseen. The rail geometry, the mono sizing and the two-column breakpoint are\nthe parts most likely to need a second pass.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>",
